WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:06.990
Explosive action scenes, a sympathetic Sherlock,
and teen romance are just some of the biggest

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:10.629
changes the Enola Holmes movie made when adapting
the original novels.

00:00:10.629 --> 00:00:15.779
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video we're exploring 11 major differences

00:00:15.779 --> 00:00:20.180
between the Netflix movie and Nancy Springer's
Enola Holmes book, The Case of the Missing

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:21.310
Marquess.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:23.990
Film and book spoilers ahead, so take care.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:28.320
During interviews for the movie, Henry Cavill
has spoken about how his Sherlock diverges

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.360
from what he calls
"the traditional misogynistic genius" we're

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.020
used to seeing as
"Enola softens [him] and opens up his heart."

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:39.480
This is very different from the way Sherlock's
portrayed in Springer's first Enola Holmes

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:40.800
mystery as well.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.110
In the book, the world famous detective certainly
has a low opinion of women,

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:49.200
banging on about their irrationality, untrustworthiness,
the "innate untidiness" of their minds,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.719
and even his own sister Enola's limited "cranial
capacity".

00:00:54.719 --> 00:00:59.220
And book-Sherlock certainly never reminisces
about his memories of Enola's childhood and

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.969
her pine-cone dog Dash, as Cavill does in
the film.

00:01:02.969 --> 00:01:07.840
Making Sherlock more openly sympathetic towards
his sister in the movie, and giving him, as

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.249
Cavill says,
"more warmth, kindness, and humanity"

00:01:10.249 --> 00:01:12.420
"You've become quite the detective Enola."

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:16.569
creates a sharper contrast between him and
Mycroft than in the novel where the brothers

00:01:16.569 --> 00:01:19.649
are pretty much as curt and imperious as each
other,

00:01:19.649 --> 00:01:23.999
both passing plenty of judgement on Enola,
her mother, and how they live.

00:01:23.999 --> 00:01:28.399
And, although we do see some similarities
between Sherlock and Enola in the novel,

00:01:28.399 --> 00:01:31.289
with her even deliberating mimicking him a
few times,

00:01:31.289 --> 00:01:35.939
the fact that in some ways they're like two
peas in a pod, as Cavill says, is emphasised

00:01:35.939 --> 00:01:37.549
more in the film.

00:01:37.549 --> 00:01:41.979
Sherlock even ends up wanting to be his sister's
guardian, which really accelerates the way

00:01:41.979 --> 00:01:47.459
he grows to respect Enola which happens much
later in the series of six books.

00:01:47.459 --> 00:01:52.520
The movie adds lots more action and intrigue
to the already very enjoyable original story.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:57.760
For example, Enola and Tewksbury cross paths
in much different circumstances in the film

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.520
than in the book
where they only meet three-quarters of the

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:05.149
way through the story when they're kidnapped
and tied up on a boat in London before escaping

00:02:05.149 --> 00:02:07.579
and being chased through the streets of the
capital.

00:02:07.579 --> 00:02:12.730
In the movie, however, Tewksbury pops up much
earlier, a mere 20 minutes into the film's

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.840
2-hour run time,
when he tumbles out of a carpetbag and then

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.610
he and Enola end up being pursued by a man
trying to murder him before they make a daring

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.060
escape from a moving train.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:27.310
Tewksbury's early on-screen introduction brings
the mystery of his disappearance forward in

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:31.640
the story and means that he and Enola spend
much more time together than in the book

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:37.060
where Enola travels alone to London by train
dressed as a widow rather than as a young

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:38.060
boy.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:41.080
The film also adds masses of intrigue to Tewsbury's
tale.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.260
In the original story, Tewky, as his mother
calls him, hates being treated like a baby

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:49.250
and wants to run away to sea,
so leaves for London where two men kidnap

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:53.730
him to get ransom money from his mum and dad,
both of whom are still alive.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:57.250
Unlike the movie,
there's no big conspiracy involving his grandmother

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:01.890
trying to have her progressive son and grandson
murdered to prevent them from increasing voting

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:04.320
rights among the general population.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.600
By adding the grandmother's scheming to the
plot,

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.750
the film raises the stakes putting Tewksbury
and Enola's life in mortal danger on several

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:16.980
occasions and also having the fate of the
country's progress rest in their hands.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:20.890
Extra drama is also added to the story of
Enola's mother in the film.

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:25.360
Eudoria is described as a "free thinker [and]
a proponent of female suffrage" in the book,

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:31.370
but there's no gunpowder plot or any clues
to a store of explosives in Limehouse Lane.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:35.610
And although Enola is attacked and kidnapped
on the streets of London, there's no attempted

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:40.140
drowning or literally explosive fight scene
like in the movie.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:44.020
Another interesting difference is that in
the book one of Enola and Tewksbury's London

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:49.370
kidnappers actually plays a dual role
as he also masquerades as a female medium

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:54.640
who first claims she can help Tewksbury's
family find their missing son and later recommends

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.070
they pay the large ransom when the demand
arrives.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:02.709
The film sees Mycroft pack Enola off to his
friend Miss Harrison's Finishing School for

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:07.530
Young Ladies, where she's taught how to walk,
speak, laugh, and sew, as well as to act and

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.160
think as she's told.

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:13.201
This is another change from the books where
there's no Miss Harrison character and Enola

00:04:13.201 --> 00:04:15.040
never goes to boarding school.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:20.499
Though book-Mycroft does arrange to send Enola
away to school to correct her wild ways,

00:04:20.499 --> 00:04:25.030
Enola tricks everyone into thinking she's
leaving for school, then runs away to continue

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.190
her search for her missing mother instead.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:32.499
Right at the start of the movie, Enola turns
to the camera and speaks directly to us in

00:04:32.499 --> 00:04:34.279
the audience:
"Now, where to begin?"

00:04:34.279 --> 00:04:38.770
While in the novel, she doesn't talk straight
to us readers in the same way, the story is

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:40.730
told from her point of view.

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:44.370
So the film's choice to have her break the
fourth wall throughout, giving us a running

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:48.979
commentary on her adventures, is a nice way
of bringing to life how the book is told through

00:04:48.979 --> 00:04:50.419
Enola's eyes.

00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:54.419
And characters connecting directly with their
audiences in that way is something director

00:04:54.419 --> 00:05:00.150
Harry Bradbeer certainly has plenty of experience
with from his work on the TV series Fleabag,

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:04.680
where creator and star Phoebe Waller-Bridge
regularly tears down the fourth wall.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:08.930
In fact, it was while watching Fleabag that
screenwriter Jack Thorne got the idea to use

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:12.840
that device in the movie as a way to
"get inside the mind of someone who spends

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.009
the majority of [her] time alone."

00:05:15.009 --> 00:05:19.389
In Bradbeer's mind, the main reason to break
the fourth wall was Enola's loneliness as

00:05:19.389 --> 00:05:23.629
that "meant that we were her only friend,
she needed to communicate with us."

00:05:23.629 --> 00:05:28.460
A striking difference between the original
story and the film is the way the movie plays

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:31.530
up a blossoming romance between Enola and
Tewksbury,

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:36.979
who's aged up from 12 years old in the book
where there's no real hint of any such crush.

00:05:36.979 --> 00:05:40.639
In the film, Tewksbury even knows Enola's
true identity;

00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:44.409
whereas in the book, she deliberately never
tells him who she really is,

00:05:44.409 --> 00:05:47.910
and he only discovers she's related to Sherlock
Holmes by accident.

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:52.849
Book-Enola even disappears from Tewksbury's
life without so much as a goodbye as she suddenly

00:05:52.849 --> 00:05:55.509
has to avoid being caught by her brother.

00:05:55.509 --> 00:05:59.930
She does "rather regret having to leave Tewky
so abruptly" and there are pointers towards

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:03.860
a possible friendship between the two
and little hints at their similarities like

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:07.550
their reasons for running away and the hard
lessons they learn about the lives of the

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:08.910
poor in London.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:13.490
But there's nothing in the novel like Tewksbury's
invitation for Enola to live with his family,

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:17.349
or their parting kiss,
or any of the clear signals that they're very

00:06:17.349 --> 00:06:22.389
much kindred spirits and that Louis Partridge's
Tewksbury will pop up again in the new film

00:06:22.389 --> 00:06:25.650
franchise,
unlike his book counterpart who only appears

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:29.529
in the first story:
"You're not rid of me yet, Viscount Tewkesbury,

00:06:29.529 --> 00:06:31.300
Marquess of Basilwether."

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:35.689
Enola's relationship with her mother is much
closer in the film than it is in the book.

00:06:35.689 --> 00:06:40.139
In the movie, Enola tells us that they spent
a lot of time together and that her mum taught

00:06:40.139 --> 00:06:43.319
her much of what she knows, including reading,
science, and sports:

00:06:43.319 --> 00:06:46.060
"It was just the two of us, and it was wonderful."

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:50.599
In the book, however, while her mother encourages
her daughter's reading, drawing, and general

00:06:50.599 --> 00:06:53.139
independence, and even taught her to ride
a bike,

00:06:53.139 --> 00:06:58.370
Enola specifically says that "Mum and I seldom
interfered in one another's concerns."

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:02.580
Given the movie cast well-known actress Helena
Bonham Carter as Enola's mother though, it's

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:07.879
hardly surprising that the filmmakers decided
to expand on the mother-daughter relationship.

00:07:07.879 --> 00:07:13.069
And beefing up their familial bond and their
activities together also gives Enola a backstory

00:07:13.069 --> 00:07:17.719
that's more visually interesting than sitting
reading or wandering around the estate's grounds

00:07:17.719 --> 00:07:20.139
alone,
which is how she tells us she spent a lot

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:22.169
of time in the book.

00:07:22.169 --> 00:07:25.740
In the original story,
Enola attributes at least part of the emotional

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:29.659
distance between herself and her mum to the
fact she was born what she calls "indecently

00:07:29.659 --> 00:07:34.139
late" in her mother's life,
creating "a scandal, a burden".

00:07:34.139 --> 00:07:40.279
In fact, when Eudoria goes missing in the
novel, she's 64 years old, and Enola is 14.

00:07:40.279 --> 00:07:42.889
At the time of filming, Bonham Carter was
53,

00:07:42.889 --> 00:07:46.149
and while she wasn't visibly aged up to play
Eudoria,

00:07:46.149 --> 00:07:50.590
the movie does increase Enola's age slightly,
making her 16 years old,

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:55.339
which is the same age as actress Millie Bobby
Brown at the film's release in 2020.

00:07:55.339 --> 00:07:59.979
The filmmakers are very likely hoping to kick
off a franchise with this using the six books

00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:03.080
in the series,
so it makes sense for Brown not to have to

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:07.569
play a very young teenager for what could
be several years as she grows up.

00:08:07.569 --> 00:08:11.879
A significant change when it comes to the
end of the story is that there's no emotional

00:08:11.879 --> 00:08:16.110
reunion between Enola and her mum by the final
chapter of the book.

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:19.099
Instead, things are left relatively up in
the air,

00:08:19.099 --> 00:08:22.979
with Enola simply receiving a coded message
from her mum in a newspaper,

00:08:22.979 --> 00:08:26.789
which she interprets using the language of
flowers as meaning that Eudoria is living

00:08:26.789 --> 00:08:30.159
a contented life, "wandering, free" with Gypsies.

00:08:30.159 --> 00:08:35.400
In stark contrast, the movie has Eudoria turn
up unexpectedly at Enola's rented room in

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:37.210
London to explain her absence.

00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:40.270
"I couldn't bear...
to have this world be your future.

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:41.910
So I had to fight."

00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:47.030
This somewhat teary scene wraps up the mother's
story just about enough for now and possibly

00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:50.740
even enough for future films
if the filmmakers or Bonham Carter decide

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:54.950
against bringing the character back;
yet it also leaves things open enough if everyone

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:57.440
wants her to return:
"Thank you for your irises.

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:00.740
Now, if you ever...
ever need me, I'll be looking out for them."

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:05.280
Now, if like me, you can't wait for another
Enola Holmes film, there were some huge clues

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:08.950
in the movie's ending that reveal what we
can look forward to in the sequel.

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:13.180
